<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>If you're building up or out, a residential structural engineer in Perth is usually one of the first people your builder or building surveyor asks about. Any change to floor area or height falls outside WA's building-permit exemption for renovations, so a second storey, a rear extension or a granny flat needs a permit, and the permit needs structural documents behind it. Kings Park Structural Co is paid advertising for independent structural engineers: we pass your enquiry to an engineering firm, which quotes you and contracts with you directly.</p><p>Perth adds its own wrinkle. A 2025 HIA-commissioned report found WA is the only state where more than half of detached houses are brick, at 68 per cent. In a double-brick house the inner leaf of the external walls, and usually some internal walls, carry the floors, ceilings and roof, so putting a new floor on top is an engineering question before it's a design one. Call (08) 5550 6237 and the engineer who quotes your job can tell you what they need to see first.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Second storeys on double brick</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The engineer works out whether the existing walls and footings can take a new floor, and what the design has to change if they can't. Original plans help, so dig them out if you have them.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Rear extensions</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">New footings, slab and walls designed to sit beside the old house. The drawings detail where the old structure meets the new.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Alfresco and verandah roofs</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Beam, post and footing sizes for a roofed alfresco, with wind loads worked out for your site under AS/NZS 1170.2. A roofed patio is not a pergola under the permit exemptions, which trips a lot of people up.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Footings and slabs to your site class</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Footing and slab design follows the AS 2870 site class. Sand, usually Class A, is the most common Perth soil, but there are clay sites too, particularly in the Perth Hills.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Granny flats and ancillary dwellings</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Slab, footing and frame engineering for a separate small dwelling on your block. Size and setback limits are a planning question, so check those with your council first.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Paperwork your surveyor can rely on</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Signed drawings and a technical certificate listing the documents it covers. The building surveyor relies on these when signing the certificate of design compliance.</p>
      </div>
</div>
